// A CITED hole — `hole name cites "<source>": formula` — is assumed on the authority of an
// external source. It is STILL a hole: the kernel treats it as an assumption and `--axioms`
// marks it `— CITED HOLE`. But a strict `check` PASSES it, disclosing the citation instead,
// because the one thing this checker cannot do is verify that the source says what the
// citation claims.
//
// This is the honest home for a borrowed result. `axiom` is for a theory's OWN primitives
// (Peano's successor laws, the ring axioms), and relabelling someone else's theorem as a
// local axiom is how an unproved step stops being visible — so `axiom ... cites` is a hard
// parse error, and a BARE hole is still rejected. A citation buys passage, not silence.
sort Nat
const ZERO: Nat
func succ(n: Nat) => Nat
pred even(n: Nat)

// borrowed, with the source named.
hole zeroIsEven cites "Hardy & Wright, Thm 1 (parity of zero)": even(ZERO)

theorem restsOnACitedHole: even(ZERO)
proof
  @conclusion |
    even(ZERO)
    [by cite zeroIsEven]
qed
